  "prompt_text": "This figure summarizes how type I interferon broadly strengthens anti-tumor or antiviral immune responses by acting on several immune cell types at once. The central message is that type I IFN promotes immune activation and cytotoxicity while reducing immune suppression.\n\nOn the left, dendritic cells are shown becoming more mature and migrating to lymph nodes, with increased costimulatory molecules and antigen presentation through MHC. They can take up antigens from dead cells and drive cross-priming of CD8+ T cells. These CD8+ cytotoxic T lymphocytes are then depicted as more effective, with increased survival and killing capacity, and they also appear to gain protection from NK-cell attack.\n\nType I IFN also acts directly on NK cells, which are shown releasing perforin 1 and granzyme B, indicating enhanced cytotoxic function. In parallel, macrophages are stimulated toward a more inflammatory state, associated with IL-18 and IL-1β. On the far right, regulatory T cells are shown with altered PDE4 and cAMP signaling, corresponding to reduced immunosuppressive activity.\n\nOverall, the figure presents type I IFN as a coordinator of innate and adaptive immunity, boosting antigen presentation, cytotoxic killing, and inflammation while weakening suppressive Treg effects.",
